09]\n    * [Canes][110]\n    * [Crutches][111]\n    * [Walkers][112]\n  * [Home Therapy Kits][113]\n    * [Ankle][114]\n    * [Knee][115]\n    * [Shoulder][116]\n  * [Pediatric][117]\n    * [Collar][118]\n    * [Elbow][119]\n    * [Foot][120]\n    * [Hip][121]\n    * [Knee][122]\n    * [Miscellaneous][123]\n    * [Shoulder][124]\n    * [Wrist][125]\n  * [PDAC / SADMERC Letters][126]\n* [Patients][127]\n  * [Patient Overview][128]\n  * [Patient Service Centers][129]\n* [Providers][130]\n  * [Provider Overview][131]\n  * [Impact Solutions][132]\n  * [Vision with Clarity][133]\n  * [Capture][134]\n  * [BregPay][135]\n  * [Breg ProFit\u2122][136]\n  * [Become a Customer][137]\n  * [Find Your US Rep][138]\n  * [Download Product Catalog][139]\n* [Store][140]\nSearch\n\n# Keep Moving\n# Forward^{\u00ae}\n\n## Improving quality of life by providing creative solutions for joint rehabilitation and health.\n\n# Keep Moving\n# Forward^{\u00ae}\n\n## Improving quality of life by providing creative solutions for joint rehabilitation and health.\n\n## Our Mission\n\nAt Breg, we empower movement and transform patient care by delivering innovative orthopedic products\nand business solutions that enhance mobility, improve patient outcomes, and streamline operations.\nWith a commitment to excellence and customer focused innovation, we move people forward every day.\n\n## Patients\n\nSupporting recovery with products designed for mobility and comfort.\n\n[ Learn More ][141]\n\n## Providers\n\nInnovative products and solutions that enhance your clients\u2019 recovery.\n\n[ Learn More ][142]\n\n## Services\n\nA customized DME program that simplifies administration and elevates patient care.\n\n[ Learn More ][143]\n\n## Products\n\nOver 320 products, styles, and sizes to fit every orthopedic need.\n\n[ Learn More ][144]\n\n## Patient Stories\n\n#### [Game-Changing Recovery Routine][145]\n\nShaili\u2019s adventures have taken her many places, including the Mt. Everest Base Camp climb. As an\nadventurer she is fearless, but sometimes the world pushes back. A ski accident in February of 2026\nleft her with a full ACL tear and a partial MCL tear. Breg\u2019s Cold Therapy has helped her manage\nswelling, reduce pain, and stay more consistent with her recovery routine.\n[ Read More ][146]\n[ [Joey Bulla wearing knee brace] ][147]\n\n#### [Regained My Flexibility][148]\n\nJoey was actively competing in martial arts and fight contests when his patella tendon suddenly\nruptured. The pain was so intense that he was unable to walk for quite some time, putting him on the\nsidelines. His doctor performed surgery on his knee and put him in a competitor\u2019s knee brace, but\neventually switched the brace out for a Breg T Scope\u00ae Premier Post-Op Knee Brace after complaints of\nthe competitor\u2019s brace being too uncomfortable. After only three months in our brace, Joey\u2019s range\nof motion in his knee had gone from only bending 5 degrees to 110 degrees!\n[ Read More ][149]\n[ [Leah Schulze wearing brace sits on beach with friend] ][150]\n\n#### [New Hips, New Life][151]\n\nHip surgery is painful and debilitating at any age, but for Leah Schulze, torn labrums and femoral\nacetabular impingements in each of her hips forced her to consider the option at just 25 years old.\nLeading a very active lifestyle that included weight lifting, golfing, swimming, skiing and running,\nshe was hesitant to pursue surgery at such a young age, but her pathologies were beginning to really\nslow her down. When she decided it was time to have the surgeries, her surgeon instructed her to\nwear a T Scope\u00ae Post-Op Hip Brace for four weeks after each one to help\n[ Read More ][152]\n[ [Christina Esposito plays field hockey while wearing knee brace] ][153]\n\n#### [The Journey To GOING PRO][154]\n\nAt just 25 years old, Christina Esposito\u2019s knee has been through it all. It all began her freshman\nyear of college. She was at the top of her lacrosse game and earned a spot on Northwestern\nUniversity\u2019s starting line-up as an attacker. She was practicing with her teammates before a game\nday when she suddenly felt (and heard) multiple pops in her left knee. In a split second, she had\ntorn her lateral and medial meniscus, ACL and MCL, and suffered from an LCL sprain and multiple bone\ncontusions.\n[ Read More ][155]\n[ [Gina Marie Policastro lifts dumbbell overhead while wearing knee brace] ][156]\n\n#### [Training Tough: Elite Athlete Comes Back From ACL Tear][157]\n\nTeaching high school science in New York C"
